{ch:'traps',type:'mc',
 q:`A colleague adds <code>self._log = []</code> AFTER <code>super().__init__()</code> in a new device. In normal use it works. After receiving <code>*RST</code> it crashes with <code>AttributeError: \'MyDevice\' object has no attribute \'_log\'</code>. Why?`,
 opts:[
   {t:'<code>*RST</code> triggers <code>_do_reset()</code>, which the colleague implemented to call <code>self._log.clear()</code>. But <code>_log</code> was never assigned because <code>super().__init__()</code> raised an exception before reaching line 2.',correct:false,why:'super().__init__() does not raise on a missing attribute at init time. The issue is the ORDER of initialization, not an exception.'},
   {t:'<code>*RST</code> calls <code>_do_reset()</code>. If <code>_do_reset()</code> references <code>self._log</code>, it works once (after __init__ completes) but crashes if <code>*RST</code> arrives before <code>self._log = []</code> is reached — which can happen if <code>super().__init__()</code> itself triggers a reset.',correct:true,why:'Correct. If super().__init__() sends a reset signal, _do_reset() runs DURING __init__() before self._log is assigned (since the assignment comes after super()). Also, if _do_reset() is overridden to reference self._log, any call to reset() before self._log is assigned fails. Rule: all attributes referenced in _do_reset() or handlers must be set before super().'},
   {t:'<code>*RST</code> reinitialises the object by calling <code>__init__</code> again, but this time <code>super().__init__()</code> overwrites the attributes that were set after it in the first call.',correct:false,why:'*RST calls reset() → _do_reset(). It does NOT call __init__ again. The object is not re-initialised.'},
   {t:'<code>super().__init__()</code> calls <code>_do_reset()</code> as part of the IEEE 488.2 power-on reset sequence, which runs before <code>self._log = []</code> is reached in the subclass.',correct:false,why:'super().__init__() calls _setup_handlers(), not _do_reset(). _do_reset() is only called by reset(). However, if a handler registered by _setup_handlers() is invoked during __init__ for some reason, a missing attribute would also crash.'},
 ]},

{ch:'traps',type:'wr',
 q:'You name your registry key <code>"OSC"</code> but a hardware_docs JSON spec also uses <code>"OSC"</code> as its name. Both are loaded. Which one wins and why?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'first-seen wins in BLOCK_REGISTRY',terms:['first','first-seen','first seen','wins']},
   {label:'packaged scanned before user dir',terms:['packaged','packaged_dir','custom_blocks','scanned first']},
   {label:'seen_names set prevents duplicate',terms:['seen_names','seen names','duplicate','skip']},
 ],
 model:'First-seen wins. discover_specs() scans PACKAGED_DIR first, then USER_DIR. When register_custom_blocks() iterates the discovered specs, it inserts each name into a seen_names set. If "OSC" already exists in seen_names, the spec is skipped — so the packaged spec would win over a user-dir spec. Then the Python block registered directly in canvas.py (after discover_specs() runs at startup) would OVERWRITE the spec entry in BLOCK_REGISTRY because it\'s assigned directly: BLOCK_REGISTRY["OSC"] = OscilloscopeBlock. Canvas-level Python registration runs at import time, after startup discovery. Avoid name collisions by using unique, descriptive keys.',
},

{ch:'traps',type:'wr',
 q:'A block\'s <code>_paint_content()</code> calls <code>import random</code> at the top of the method body to generate noise. Is this a problem? When does it get called and how often?',
 concepts:[
   {label:'_paint_content called every repaint',terms:['every','each repaint','every repaint','each paint','called repeatedly']},
   {label:'import inside method is slow but functional',terms:['slow','cached','sys.modules','import inside']},
   {label:'should move import to top of file',terms:['top of file','module level','file top','move import']},
 ],
 model:'Functionally it works because Python caches module imports in sys.modules — the second and subsequent imports of "random" are O(1) dict lookups. But _paint_content() is called on every repaint, which can happen many times per second if the user is moving the block or other items are animating. The import lookup, while fast, is still unnecessary overhead inside a hot path. Move the import to the top of the file (module level). This is a minor style issue, not a crash — but it\'s a clean-code trap worth knowing.',
},

{ch:'traps',type:'mc',
 q:'Which of these does NOT cause the block face to visually update after a device state change triggered from a right-click menu action?',
 opts:[
   {t:'Calling <code>self.device.state.voltage = 5.0</code> directly in the lambda without calling <code>send_command()</code> first.',correct:false,why:'Setting state directly (bypassing send_command) is a valid shortcut for right-click shortcuts — it updates device state just as well. The block face still needs self.update() regardless of how state is set.'},
   {t:'Calling <code>send_command("VOLT 5.0")</code> and then <code>self.update()</code>.',correct:false,why:'send_command() updates device state; self.update() schedules a repaint. Together they correctly update the block face. This IS the pattern that works.'},
   {t:'Calling <code>send_command("VOLT 5.0")</code> without calling <code>self.update()</code>.',correct:true,why:'Correct — this does NOT cause a visual update. send_command() changes device state, but Qt has no idea the visual representation is stale. Without self.update(), the repaint is never scheduled, so the block face stays frozen at the old value until Qt independently decides to repaint.'},
   {t:'Calling <code>self.scene().update()</code> instead of <code>self.update()</code>.',correct:false,why:'scene().update() forces a full scene repaint, which is wasteful but functional — it would repaint this block along with everything else. So it DOES cause a visual update (just inefficiently).'},
 ]},

